---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/replenishment';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/replenishment');
const desc = description(site, 'use-cases/replenishment');
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16 md:py-24">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases">← All use cases</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Replenishment reminders</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Shopify SMS and email: replenishment reminders</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">Build the consumption clock at product level. This guide sets out selection criteria and proposed workflows; it does not report hands-on platform tests or merchant campaign results.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-16 space-y-8">
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Use buying cycles rather than a universal delay</h2>
      <p>Replenishment reminds a prior buyer about a product they may be ready to replace. It differs from general winback: a slow-use serum and a frequently used coffee bag should not share one arbitrary thirty-day timer. Start with observed reorder distributions and customer usage information. Email can explain quantity and options; an optional SMS reminder should add timely value rather than repeat the same offer automatically.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Supplier fit: Klaviyo replenishment filters</h2>
      <p>Klaviyo documents replenishment templates for Shopify and product-specific order filters. Its guide explains checking for no subsequent order since flow entry, scoped to the relevant product when appropriate. That distinction matters: buying a mug should not cancel a coffee reminder. Validate actual variant and subscription data in your account before relying on this design.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Build a supply map</h2>
      <p>For each SKU or timing family, record pack size, expected usage range, typical reorder interval, shipping lead time, gift exclusions and subscription source. Keep an owner and last-reviewed date. Start with a few high-repeat products. A category estimate can be a fallback when item-level history is sparse, but label uncertainty rather than claiming to know when a household runs out.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Three proposed merchant workflows</h2>
      <p>Vitamins: for a hypothetical forty-five-day supply, a draft eighty-percent reminder falls on day thirty-six, with a later check around day forty-five. Pet food: adjust for purchased quantity and reported household usage rather than assuming breed alone sets the clock. Coffee: separate bag sizes and exclude known gifts. These examples are planning exercises, not measured subscription conversion or reorder results.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Reset and suppress by product</h2>
      <p>Before each reminder check whether the relevant product was reordered, refunded or cancelled. A new purchase should reset its own clock without erasing unrelated product timers. Active subscribers should not receive a one-time reorder pitch for the item already scheduled. A paused or failed subscription needs its own service or recovery policy rather than an automatic promotional replacement order.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Handle multi-item and irregular usage</h2>
      <p>A single order may contain a fast-use consumable and a durable accessory. Send only the relevant replenishable item and consolidate reminders due close together. Gifts, seasonal use, stockpiling and pack-size changes can invalidate a timer. Allow customers to say they are not ready or choose a later reminder. Do not interpret a long reorder gap as evidence of product failure.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Protect full-price economics</h2>
      <p>Start with a useful reorder link and accurate product details rather than a discount by default. A later subscription offer should explain renewal, pause and skip terms. Calculate contribution after product cost, fulfillment, incentives and messaging fees. Choose a target from the store economics; there is no established universal sixty-percent full-price threshold. Coordinate with winback so two flows do not compete on discount depth.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Proposed acceptance exercise</h2>
      <p>Check a draft cohort with a same-product reorder, unrelated purchase, multi-pack, refund, gift and active subscription. Confirm product links and the latest subscription state just before dispatch. Verify that a repeated order event does not duplicate reminders. These account tests are proposed, not performed. Start any later live experiment with a limited eligible cohort and a clear pause rule.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Calibrate with a comparison group</h2>
      <p>Compare actual reorder timing with the supply map by product and quantity. Propose an eligible holdout to estimate incremental orders rather than crediting every natural reorder to the reminder. Track contribution, repeat-order interval, opt-outs and subscription overlap after returns. Revisit timing when pack sizes or shipping times change, and retain the prior rules so changes can be explained.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Primary supplier reference</h2>
      <p>Checked 25 November 2026: <a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360003195232">Klaviyo replenishment flow documentation</a>. The supplier fact above is limited to that documented capability. The workflow rules and acceptance exercises are editorial proposals for a merchant to validate.</p>
    </div>
    <nav class="flex flex-wrap gap-4 text-sm font-bold" aria-label="Related guides">
      <a class="link" href="/use-cases/post-purchase">Post purchase →</a>
      <a class="link" href="/use-cases/winback">Winback →</a>
      <a class="link" href="/apps">App directory →</a>
    </nav>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
